  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "For which value of $x$ does the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+27x+4$ have a local maximum? Do not round the answer.\n\nThe local maximum is at $x=$\n{{field-1}}.",
  "worked_solution": "First, we find the stationary points. Differentiating $f(x)$ gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{\u2032}(x)=-3{x}^{2}+27.\n$$\n\nSolving ${f}^{\u2032}(x)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}+27=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=\\pm 3.\n$$\n\nSo we have two stationary points, $x=\\pm 3.$ Next, to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima, we use the first derivative test.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-4,0,4$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{\u2032}(-4) & =-3(-4)^{2}+27=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(0) & =-3(0)^{2}+27=27>0 \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(4) & =-3(4)^{2}+27=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $-3$ | $(-3,3)$ | $3$ | $(3,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-4$ |  | $0$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{\u2032}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $\u2198$ | min | $\u2197$ | max | $\u2198$ |\n\nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n- At $x=-3,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-3$ is a local minimum.\n\n- At $x=3,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=3$ is a local maximum.\n\nWe conclude that $f(x)$ has one local maximum at $x=3.$",
